58,3,,status,Benjamin,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":4,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
59,3,,status,Minato,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
60,3,1,talk,Kenji,,"Looking at these, Plan B seems safest! A paved plaza means less deep digging and no worrying about clay slippage. It's way more realistic to finish on time than digging ponds or planting a whole forest!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"While Plan B is structurally safe, it may exacerbate the heat trap I discovered. However, Plan A's ponds could be a disaster given the current drainage failures and clay slippage. We need a balance of utility and resilience.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Minato,,"I think Plan B or D might be better because they have more hard surfaces. Since there are only seven groundskeepers, they won't have to work so hard to keep it beautiful.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Minako,,"Plan A and C are too risky. Given the history of dead trees and failing surfaces, dense planting and complex water features will likely become ruins. Plan B or D are more realistic for our capacity.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Jonathan,,Plan B is the most fiscally responsible. It avoids the high maintenance costs of planted areas and doesn't rely on the habitat fund to mask a future deficit. It is a sustainable choice for the city's budget.,"{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Kenji,,"@Benjamin, you're right about the heat, but Plan D's terraces might need a lot of digging into that clay! Plan B is just a flat plaza, which is much easier to build and finish quickly.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Kenji, a flat plaza is indeed simpler, but we must not forget the tram commuters. Plan B's open layout likely accommodates those desire lines far better than the structured courts of Plan D.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Minato,,"@Benjamin, that's a good point. If the plaza is open, people can walk where they need to without hurting any plants. I think Plan B is the kindest choice for the few workers we have.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Minako,,"@Minato, I agree. Plan B minimizes the risk of negligent aftercare causing a visible failure. It is far better to have a durable plaza than a woodland that dies in two years due to poor contracts.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Minako, I completely agree. Plan B avoids the trap of using the habitat fund to build something that will eventually bankrupt the maintenance budget. It is the only option that ensures long-term financial peace for the city.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Kenji,,I'm totally sold on Plan B! It's the only one that doesn't sound like a nightmare for the construction crews. No deep ponds or terraces means we can actually get it done without the clay causing a disaster!,"{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,It is rare that I find myself in such complete agreement with Kenji's enthusiasm. Plan B is the only logical choice; it respects the pedestrians' paths and avoids the clay's volatility. I shall vote for B.,"{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Minato,,I will vote for Plan B too. It seems like the best way to make sure the few groundskeepers and the kind volunteers don't get too tired. It's a gentle plan for everyone.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Minako,,"I will also vote for Plan B. It is the only option that prioritizes durability over aesthetics, ensuring the park remains functional and intact long after the initial opening. It is the most resilient choice.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Jonathan,,I concur. Plan B is the only option that aligns with the city's long-term financial health by minimizing high-cost maintenance. It is the most sustainable path forward. I shall also vote for Plan B.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Kenji,,"It's awesome that we all agree! Plan B is definitely the way to go. It's simple, safe, and we can actually finish it! I'm voting for Plan B!","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,"A rare moment of total consensus. It appears logic and pragmatism have triumphed over architectural fantasy. I cast my vote for Plan B, trusting it will survive both the elements and the people.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Minato,,"I'm happy we all agree. I'll vote for Plan B too, so the people taking care of the park can stay happy and healthy. I hope everyone enjoys the new plaza!","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Minako,,"My vote is for Plan B. It is the only choice that avoids the cycle of grand openings followed by rapid decay. By choosing durability, we ensure a lasting legacy for the city.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Jonathan,,My vote is for Plan B. It is the only choice that ensures the city's financial stability by avoiding unsustainable maintenance costs. I am glad we have found a path to long-term peace.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
